{"name":"Luscious Lemongrass","hex":"#517933","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"luscious","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Sweet and pleasant; delicious; juicy, succulent.","Sexually appealing; seductive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/luscious"},{"term":"lemongrass","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of various species of grass of the genus Cymbopogon, especially Cymbopogon citratus, which have a lemon-like taste and aroma, and are used in cooking, for tea, and for fragrance.","Sourgrass (Oxalis pes-caprae)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lemongrass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cymbopogon","blocks":[{"text":"Cymbopogon, also known as lemongrass, barbed wire grass, silky heads, oily heads, Cochin grass, Malabar grass, citronella grass or fever grass, is a genus of Asian, African, Australian, and tropical island plants in the grass family.\nSome species are commonly cultivated as culinary and medicinal herbs because of their scent, resembling that of lemons .\nThe name Cymbopogon derives from the Greek words kymbe and pogon \"which mean [that] in most species, the hairy spikelets project from boat-shaped spathes.\" Lemongrass and its oil are believed to possess therapeutic properties."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/YosriNov04Pokok_Serai.JPG/330px-YosriNov04Pokok_Serai.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cymbopogon","wikidata":"Q5727732"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Cymbopogon_citratus_leaves.jpg/500px-Cymbopogon_citratus_leaves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Cymbopogon_citratus_leaves.jpg/1280px-Cymbopogon_citratus_leaves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://th